<main class="sheet">
  <p class="headline">Compile-time verification for your code, and for what people experience.</p>
  <p class="lede">Rust made memory bugs compile errors. Cairn is research into doing the same for
    behaviour: everything a person can do is a <em>claim</em> with a durable id, and if its proof
    fails, the app doesn't build.</p>

  <div class="start">
    <a class="go px" href="/docs/guide/first-app/">Get started</a>
    <a href="/docs/">Read the docs</a>
    <a href="/docs/reference/releases/">v{{ $utils.version() }}</a>
  </div>

  <h2 id="what-a-claim-is">What a claim is</h2>
  <p>A claim is one statement about the app under one id, with a half for people and a half for the
    checker. Here is the whole of Reset in Counter, one of the <a href="/docs/examples/">example apps</a>:</p>

  <figure class="claim anatomy">
<pre class="px"><code><span class="ln"><span class="kw">move</span> <span class="id br" id="c-id">^2</span> reset <span class="op">(</span>s <span class="op">:</span> State<span class="op">)</span> <span class="str">"Reset"</span> <span class="br" id="c-place"><span class="kw">in</span> <span class="id">^3</span></span> <span class="kw">where</span><i class="mk">1</i><i class="mk">2</i></span><span class="ln people" id="c-people">  <span class="kw">does</span> <span class="str">"Press Reset."</span><i class="mk">3</i></span><span class="ln people">  <span class="kw">sees</span> <span class="str">"The count is zero, and Reset disappears."</span></span><span class="ln checker" id="c-checker">  <span class="kw">enabled</span> <span class="op">:=</span> <span class="br" id="c-enabled">resetEnabled s</span><i class="mk">4</i><i class="mk">5</i></span><span class="ln checker">  <span class="kw">on</span> press</span><span class="ln checker">    <span class="kw">apply</span> <span class="op">:=</span> <span class="br" id="c-apply"><span class="op">{</span> s <span class="kw">with</span> count <span class="op">:=</span> <span class="lit">0</span> <span class="op">}</span></span><i class="mk">6</i></span><span class="ln checker">    <span class="kw">post</span> s' <span class="op">:=</span> <span class="br" id="c-post">s'.count <span class="op">=</span> <span class="lit">0</span> <span class="op">∧</span> resetEnabled s' <span class="op">=</span> <span class="lit">false</span></span><i class="mk">7</i></span><span class="ln checker">    <span class="kw">sound</span> <span class="op">:=</span> <span class="br" id="c-sound"><span class="tac">by</span> <span class="tac">intro</span> s _<span class="op">;</span> <span class="tac">exact</span> <span class="op">⟨</span><span class="tac">rfl</span>, <span class="tac">rfl</span><span class="op">⟩</span></span><i class="mk">8</i></span></code></pre>
    <ol class="callouts">
      <li class="l" data-for="c-id"><b>The id</b> Permanent: never renumbered or reused, and what reviews,
        docs and agents cite. <i>Like a data-testid that can't drift.</i></li>
      <li class="r" data-for="c-place"><b>Where it lives</b> <code>^3</code> is a claim too, a place: Counter's window.</li>
      <li class="l people" data-for="c-people"><b>For people</b> <code>does</code> and <code>sees</code>, in plain
        English for reviewers and the claim's page. Lean doesn't check them.</li>
      <li class="l checker" data-for="c-checker"><b>For the checker</b> Everything below. Lean checks it when the app builds.</li>
      <li class="l" data-for="c-enabled"><b>When it's offered</b> The button is proved on screen exactly when
        this holds, in every state. <i>Playwright's toBeVisible() checks one page load.</i></li>
      <li class="r" data-for="c-apply"><b>What it does</b> A pure copy of <code>s</code> with the count at 0.</li>
      <li class="r" data-for="c-post"><b>The promise</b> Afterwards the count is zero and Reset is gone.
        <i>An expect() stated for every state, not asserted on one run.</i></li>
      <li class="r" data-for="c-sound"><b>The proof</b> <i>Like assert_eq!, except the compiler settles it for
        every s.</i> No proof, no build.</li>
    </ol>
  </figure>

  <p>Because the update is built only from moves, two things hold for every app, proved once in
    the framework: an action that no claim owns changes nothing and asks nothing of the host
    (<code>update_unclaimed</code>), and every effect the app does ask for comes from a move
    (<code>update_effect</code>).</p>

  <h3 class="sub" id="ids-are-the-contract">Ids are the contract</h3>
  <p>An id is a data-testid that can't drift, and semver for guarantees. <code>^2</code> means Reset
    for as long as the counter exists: never renumbered or reused, and a claim that goes away is
    retired with a reason. <code>claims.lock</code> records every id with a hash of its statement; the
    build fails when an id vanishes or changes kind, and warns when a statement's meaning changes. A
    repeated control carries a key (<code>^5#12</code> is <code>^5</code> for item 12), and each
    package numbers in its own scope (<code>cairn^N</code> for Cairn's own).</p>

  <h3 class="sub" id="six-kinds">Six kinds</h3>
  <table class="kinds">
    <thead><tr><th>Kind</th><th>What it says</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>move</td><td>Something a person can do, what follows, and the proof.</td><td>^1 Increment, ^2 Reset</td></tr>
      <tr><td>flow</td><td>A goal and a path of moves that reaches it. Lean checks every step is really answered.</td><td>^4 Count to three, ^5 Start over</td></tr>
      <tr><td>invariant</td><td>A property every move keeps, so it holds in every state the app can reach.</td><td>yap^311 A shortcut never sends (Yap)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>assume</td><td>Something the proofs rely on but can't prove. Recorded, and visible in every theorem that uses it.</td><td>cairn^7 The host reports clicks where they happen</td></tr>
      <tr><td>place</td><td>A region of the view where moves appear.</td><td>^3 Counter window, in the <a href="#what-a-claim-is">figure above</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>retire</td><td>A tombstone: the claim is gone, the id stays issued.</td><td>^6 No press left in progress: the runtime owns presses now</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3 class="sub" id="how-sure-we-are">How sure we are</h3>
  <p>Every claim carries its evidence, and its claim page shows which kind. Strongest first:</p>
  <ul class="trail ladder">
    <li><b>Proved.</b> Lean's kernel checked the proof, using only the three standard axioms;
      <code>make check</code> audits every theorem.</li>
    <li><b>Fuzzed.</b> Random sequences of moves, with the claim checked after every step:
      property-based testing, like proptest or QuickCheck, over whole sessions.</li>
    <li><b>Tested.</b> The real app, driven through generated sequences of moves, agrees with the
      model after every step: an end-to-end suite whose expected values come from the proved model.</li>
    <li><b>Seen.</b> The claim's control is on a captured frame, and a flow replays click by
      click.</li>
    <li><b>Assumed.</b> Recorded, never proved. Theorems that rely on it take it as a hypothesis,
      so every use shows.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Each one rules out something different and still trusts something:
    <a href="/docs/reference/trust/">how much to trust a claim</a> says what, with a bug the tests
    miss and the proof catches.</p>
  <p>Every package on the registry gets claim pages: every claim it makes, grouped by place, with
    its proofs, generated from what it published and nothing written by hand. See the example apps'
    for <a href="/packages/cairn_examples/claims/3/">Counter</a>,
    <a href="/packages/cairn_examples/claims/106/">Basics</a> and
    <a href="/packages/cairn_examples/claims/201/">TodoMVC</a>, and Cairn's for its
    <a href="/packages/cairn/claims/#assumptions">trusted base</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="the-idea">The idea</h2>
  <p>A cairn is a stack of stones that marks a trail: placed by hand, built to last, and trusted by
    whoever comes next. Here the trails are a product's flows, and the stones are claims about them:
    “pressing <code>^12</code> clears the completed items”, “<code>^12</code>'s button is visible
    and on top whenever there's something to clear”. Each claim is backed by a Lean proof, a model
    check or a conformance run against the real app.</p>
  <p>To make those claims mean something, the code they talk about has to be the code that runs.
    So the app's state, view, layout, hit testing, focus and text editing are all pure Lean, and
    the theorems are about exactly those functions.</p>
  <figure class="diagram">
{{{ $utils.architecture() }}}
    <figcaption>One frame, from a key press to the screen.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <aside class="note"><div class="card px">
    <p class="label"><img src="/icon.svg" alt="">Not a layer on top</p>
    <p>Cairn isn't verification bolted onto an existing UI framework. It is its own framework: the
      app, its view, layout, hit testing, focus and text editing are written in Lean, and a thin
      Swift host hands over events and draws the result with Metal.</p>
    <p>Owning every layer is what lets a claim reach all the way down. Each one is bound to the code
      that actually runs, not to a model of someone else's, and the little we do have to trust,
      the host, is written down in one place.</p>
  </div></aside>

